      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The power of a vote"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "On election day millions stand in queues, each with one vote of equal value. Elections let ordinary people choose who will govern them."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "EVMs and laws",
          "Election Commission",
          "challenges",
          "constituencies",
          "first past the post",
          "informed voters",
          "universal franchise",
          "why elections"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Elections are essential to democracy because they let citizens choose and change their representatives peacefully.\nIn India the country is divided into constituencies, and each elects one member to the Lok Sabha.\nThe usual system is first past the post: the candidate with the most votes wins.\nAll adult citizens aged 18 and above can vote. This is universal adult franchise.\nThe Election Commission of India is an independent body that conducts elections and enforces the Model Code of Conduct.\nElectronic voting machines record votes, and rules and laws help ensure free and fair elections.\nChallenges include money power, misleading information and low participation.\nVoters can make elections better by being informed and voting responsibly."
        }
      },
